## Defining Equality

```xiom
pub interface PartialEq {
  fn eq(self: &Self, other: &Self) -> Bool;
  fn ne(self: &Self, other: &Self) -> Bool {
    return !self.eq(other);  // Default: opposite of eq
  }
}

pub interface Eq: PartialEq {
  // No new methods — just a marker that equality is reflexive
}
```

### Implementing Eq

```xiom
pub type Point = { x: Int; y: Int; }

impl PartialEq for Point {
  fn eq(self: &Point, other: &Point) -> Bool {
    return self.x == other.x && self.y == other.y;
  }
}

impl Eq for Point {}  // Marker: equality is total

fn main() {
  let a = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 };
  let b = Point{ x: 1, y: 2 };
  let c = Point{ x: 3, y: 4 };
  io.println((a == b).to_str());  // true
  io.println((a != c).to_str());  // true
}
```

### PartialEq vs Eq

PartialEq allows partial equivalence (e.g., Float64 NaN != NaN). Eq requires FULL equivalence (reflexive, symmetric, transitive).

### Eq in Practice

```xiom
fn find_user(users: &Vec[User], target: &User) -> Option[Int] {
  for i in 0..users.len() {
    if users[i] == target { return Some(i); }  // Only works if User: Eq
  }
  return None();
}
```

